asure specialization.","opened":true},{"id":"rem-real-and-complex-normed-space-convention","used_for":"The complex scalar-field convention for bounded linear and bilinear maps.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-baire-category-for-complete-metric-spaces","used_for":"The bounded-below closed-range theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-banach-series-criterion","used_for":"Completeness of normed quotients.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-binomial-closed-formula","used_for":"Central-binomial, Chebyshev, and Bertrand estimates.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-binomial-theorem","used_for":"Central-binomial, Chebyshev, and Bertrand estimates.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-chebyshev-markov-inequality-for-the-integral","used_for":"Sigma-finite supports and the countable-cocountable counterexample.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-countable-union-of-countable","used_for":"The countable-cocountable measure construction.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-direct-comparison-test","used_for":"Bounding the prime-power tail in Mertens' first theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-dominated-convergence","used_for":"Finite-measure representation and sigma-finite duality limit passages.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-extreme-value-metric","used_for":"The oscillation-norm example.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-finite-products-of-banach-spaces-are-banach","used_for":"Coordinate product examples.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-gamma-weierstrass-product","used_for":"The Gamma-side constant identification in Mertens' third theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-holder-inequality-for-integrals","used_for":"Integrability and the pairing bound for $\\Lambda_g$.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-induction-principle","used_for":"Chebyshev and Bertrand product bounds.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind","used_for":"Unit-interval examples and point evaluation.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-linear-kernel-image-and-injectivity","used_for":"The quotient-by-kernel example.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one","used_for":"Linearity of pairings and simple-function identities.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral","used_for":"Partial summation and Mertens estimates.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-metric-closure-characterisation","used_for":"The quotient seminorm closedness criterion.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-metric-regularity-hierarchy","used_for":"Bounded-linear-operator equivalences.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral","used_for":"$L^q$ density and sigma-finite duality arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-natural-logarithm-laws","used_for":"Chebyshev, Bertrand, and Mertens logarithmic calculations.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-p-series-real-exponents","used_for":"Bounding the prime-power tail in Mertens' first theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-quotient-vector-space-universal-property","used_for":"Normed quotient factorization and the quotient-by-kernel example.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-radon-nikodym-density-exists-and-is-unique-up-to-almost-everywhere-equality","used_for":"Finite-measure duality representation.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-the-l-p-norm-descends-to-the-quotient-and-makes-l-p-a-normed-space","used_for":"$L^p$ functional norms and finite-exponent uniqueness.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality","used_for":"Well-defined $L^p$ pairings.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-von-mangoldt-divisor-sum-identity","used_for":"The von Mangoldt harmonic estimate.","opened":true}],"concerns":[{"id":"thm-an-l-q-function-defines-a-bounded-linear-functional-on-l-p","concern":"[L2] says Hölder applies to $u\\in L^p(\\mu)$ and $v\\in L^q(\\mu)$, but the cited theorem's finite-exponent clause is for measurable representatives in $\\mathcal L^p$ and $\\mathcal L^q$. Step 1.1 then applies that misstated fact to chosen representatives, so the cited domain is ill-typed as written.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"prop-lambda-g-has-operator-norm-equal-to-the-l-q-norm","concern":"[L3] adds that every set $\\{|g|>\\|g\\|_\\infty-\\varepsilon\\}$ has positive measure. The cited proposition supplies least-essential-bound properties, not this assertion; literally it fails on a zero-measure space when $g=0$. The proof's earlier zero-class branch avoids that case, but the stated fact remains overstrong.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"lem-rn-densities-of-bounded-l-p-functionals-belong-to-l-q","concern":"Step 3.1 invokes dominated convergence to prove $\\|[w_n]-[f]\\|_p\\to0$, but the item has no dominated-convergence dependency or fact. Its [L3] is monotone convergence, which is not the theorem used there.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"lem-chebyshev-psi-prime-power-expansion","concern":"Step 3.1 applies [L3] to $\\theta(x^{1/k})$ for every $k\\ge1$, while [L3] is stated only for arguments at least $2$. The definition's empty-sum remark resolves the values below $2$, but it is not supplied by [L3].","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-chebyshev-theta-linear-bounds","concern":"Step 1.2 derives the valuation-layer estimate $\\log\\binom{2n}{n}\\le\\theta(2n)+R_n$ from [L2], but [L2] as restated gives only interval divisibility and an upper bound on $v_p$, not the displayed valuation expansion needed for that derivation. The cited lemma itself contains that expansion.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-extension-of-a-bounded-map-from-a-dense-subspace","concern":"In step 5.1 the strict bound $\\|Td_n(x)\\|<C(\\|x\\|+1/n)$ is invalid when the permitted bound is $C=0$. A one-line separate argument that $T=0$, hence its extension is zero, closes the case.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"lem-bounded-functionals-on-finite-l-p-define-finite-signed-or-complex-measures","concern":"[L1] attributes continuity to the bounded-functional definition, although that definition states linearity and a norm inequality only. Step 3.1 needs continuity; it follows immediately from the bound but is not stated or derived there.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"}],"alerts":[],"seams_checked":[]}